# ~/.zshenv — private environment for every zsh invocation.
#
# setup.sh seeds ~/.dotfiles/zshenv.local from this sample, chmods it to 600,
# and symlinks it to ~/.zshenv. zshenv.local is gitignored, so your real
# values never get committed. Edit ~/.dotfiles/zshenv.local directly; this
# sample just stays in the repo as a reference.
#
# zsh reads .zshenv on EVERY invocation: login and interactive shells, but
# also `zsh -c ...` from scripts, editors, cron, and tools like Claude Code.
# That reach is the point — it's the only way to get vars into non-interactive
# shells — but it comes with two rules:
#
#   1. Keep it SILENT. Anything written to stdout here (an echo, a command
#      that prints) corrupts non-interactive sessions: scp / rsync / sftp to a
#      zsh host break with cryptic errors. Redirect command noise with
#      2>/dev/null and send any diagnostics you need to stderr (>&2).
#
#   2. Keep it FAST and PATH-independent. .zshenv runs before /etc/zprofile,
#      Homebrew's shellenv, and ~/.zshrc, so PATH is still bare — e.g.
#      /opt/homebrew/bin is NOT on it yet. `security` works (it's in
#      /usr/bin); `op`, `aws`, `gpg` and friends usually are NOT found here.
#
# This file is for static secrets that every shell needs. For secrets that
# only interactive shells use — anything needing `op`, a prompt, or a full
# PATH — use secrets.local instead (sourced from profile). For non-secret
# machine config (aliases, proxies, overrides), use profile.local.
#
# .zshenv runs first, so anything exported here can be overridden later by
# secrets.local / profile / profile.local — set a value in one place, not two.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Plain export  (simplest; the recommended default)
#
# Plaintext on disk, but outside the repo and chmod 600. Fast, silent, and
# PATH-independent — everything this file wants to be.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# export LINEAR_API_KEY="your-linear-api-key"
# export UPTIME_ROBOT_KEY="your-uptime-robot-key"
# export UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY="your-unsplash-access-key"
# export GH_TOKEN="your-github-personal-access-token"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. macOS Keychain  (secret is not stored in a dotfile)
#
# `security` lives in /usr/bin, so it works even from the bare PATH .zshenv
# runs with. The cost is one subprocess per key per shell — fine for a
# handful, noticeable if you spawn shells in a hot loop.
#
# Store once. Add -T /usr/bin/security so reads via the CLI don't trigger the
# "security wants to use the login keychain" prompt:
#   security add-generic-password -a "$USER" -s "linear-api" -w "your-key" -T /usr/bin/security
#
# Assign first, then export only if non-empty, so a missing entry can't
# clobber the variable with "":
#   _v="$(security find-generic-password -a "$USER" -s "linear-api" -w 2>/dev/null)"
#   [ -n "$_v" ] && export LINEAR_API_KEY="$_v"; unset _v
#
# If per-shell lookups start to drag: resolve once into a chmod 600 cache
# file with a timestamp, and only refresh when it's older than some TTL.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1Password (`op`) does NOT work here — it's under /opt/homebrew/bin, which
# isn't on PATH this early, it would run on every shell spawn, and it can pop
# an unlock prompt mid-script. Put `op`-backed secrets in secrets.local
# instead (sourced from profile, interactive shells only). See
# secrets.local.sample.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
